{
  "id": "50m6jyy",
  "title": "Chapter 9: SYNTHETIC FIBRES AND PLASTICS",
  "order": 29,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "loly7s2",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1. Synthetic Fibres"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Artificial Silk",
          "value": "Rayon",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "1st Fully Synthetic Fibre",
          "value": "Nylon",
          "icon": "award"
        },
        {
          "label": "PET Resin Code",
          "value": "1",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "Non-Stick Coating",
          "value": "Teflon",
          "icon": "check"
        }
      ],
      "id": "7u6gu5s"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6jxf6iu",
      "content": "Synthetic fibres are man-made fibres prepared from petrochemicals.They are made by polymerisation process."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "75y3r52",
      "content": "Synthetic fibres are strong, elastic and wrinkle-resistant."
    },
    {
      "id": "kd5addj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Types of Synthetic Fibres"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9h1fom9",
      "content": "• Rayon – Artificial silk made from cellulose."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pkiy1ez",
      "content": "• Nylon – First fully synthetic fibre."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "y82kyil",
      "content": "• Polyester – Used in clothes and bottles (PET)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2g08pkx",
      "content": "• Acrylic – Used as wool substitute."
    },
    {
      "id": "csp25nv",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "2. Plastics"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zn5rl21",
      "content": "Plastics are polymers made from petrochemicals."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0kx5iiw",
      "content": "They are non-reactive, light, strong and durable."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ld28na0",
      "content": "Plastics are poor conductors of electricity."
    },
    {
      "id": "m5rax0w",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Types of Plastics"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "49rg6af",
      "content": "Thermoplastics – Can be reshaped on heating (e.g., PVC, Polythene)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "a7ls3if",
      "content": "Thermosetting plastics – Cannot be reshaped once set (e.g., Bakelite, Melamine)."
    },
    {
      "id": "fc584sd",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "3. Polymerisation"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gcbomue",
      "content": "Polymerisation is the process of joining small molecules (monomers) to form large molecules (polymers)."
    },
    {
      "id": "mheug2g",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "4. Advantages of Plastics"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "a7cnamt",
      "content": "• Light weight"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "xhjo85a",
      "content": "• Strong and durable"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "hoxle7j",
      "content": "• Corrosion resistant"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "8jy59v0",
      "content": "• Cheap"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Nylon was the first fully synthetic fibre, created entirely from coal, water, and air without using any natural raw materials from plants or animals.",
      "id": "x70vi57"
    },
    {
      "id": "hijt534",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "5. Disadvantages of Plastics"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "1ubrt8x",
      "content": "• Non-biodegradable"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "rt6ela1",
      "content": "• Cause environmental pollution"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "yk927ep",
      "content": "• Harm marine life"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "g25pk93",
      "content": "<strong>6. Biodegradable vs Non-Biodegradable</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2zufyek",
      "content": "Biodegradable substances can be decomposed by microorganisms."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "rmpkx9l",
      "content": "Non-biodegradable substances cannot be decomposed easily."
    },
    {
      "id": "iqo8usl",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "TABLE SECTION"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Rayon is synthesized from wood pulp and is commonly referred to as artificial silk.",
        "Nylon was the first fully synthetic fibre, manufactured entirely from coal, water, and air without any natural raw materials.",
        "Polymers are large macromolecular structures formed by linking repeating chemical units called monomers through polymerisation.",
        "Thermoplastics deform and melt easily upon heating, allowing them to be remolded, whereas thermosetting plastics set permanently.",
        "Plastics are non-biodegradable, posing severe environmental hazards due to long-term persistence in soil and water bodies."
      ],
      "id": "cf9tqps"
    },
